{"page_content": "ACME/Flame Design Final Report (2023) \n \n6 April 2023 \n35 \n \n \nand fuel mixed with nitrogen.   In the latter case, the ACME hardware allows for \nsimultaneous flow of fuel (from a launched bottle) and ISS nitrogen to the burner, \nallowing for on-orbit fuel dilution and testing at a continuum of fuel concentrations. \n \nNo samples (e.g., of soot) were collected for Flame Design; all of the ISS results were \nelectronically transmitted to the ground.  That is furthermore the case for all currently \nplanned ACME experiments. \n \nA summary log of the flames ignited during Increments 59-60, including a limited \noverview of the test conditions, is found in Table 1.  The table includes a limited overview \nof the test conditions.  For completeness, a row was included for testing on June 10 (GMT \n161) even though it was quickly discovered that the hot-wire igniter tip had failed at the \nend of preceding test day.  As a result, flames were not ignited until ten days later after \nastronaut Christina Koch had replaced the igniter tip.", "metadata": {"source": "PSI-10_Reports_FinalRpt_ACMEFlameDesign.pdf", "page": 35}}